1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,119
ChatGPT once made up court

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:02,560
cases.

3
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:03,720
Not one.

4
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:05,180
A whole list.

5
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:07,699
And a real lawyer handed them to

6
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:08,380
a real judge.

7
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:10,660
The judge looked them up.

8
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:12,080
They didn't exist.

9
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:14,359
And when the lawyer asked it, is

10
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:16,340
this case real, it said yes.

11
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:19,079
Last time, we saw that AI

12
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:21,079
guesses what sounds right, not

13
00:00:21,080 --> 00:00:21,620
what is right.

14
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:23,600
Here, those two split apart.

15
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:25,680
Why would it do that?

16
00:00:26,740 --> 00:00:27,940
Let's un-jargon it.

17
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:30,880
It wasn't lying.

18
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:33,060
Lying means you know the truth.

19
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:35,379
It was guessing, like a student

20
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:37,040
bluffing in a closed book exam.

21
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:39,659
When an AI makes something up

22
00:00:39,660 --> 00:00:41,519
and says it like a fact, that's

23
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:42,660
called a hallucination.

24
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:44,659
Odd word, because it isn't

25
00:00:44,660 --> 00:00:45,300
seeing things.

26
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:46,640
It's bluffing.

27
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:49,419
Picture that student stuck on a

28
00:00:49,420 --> 00:00:49,780
question.

29
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:51,959
Leave it blank and that's zero

30
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:52,420
points.

31
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:54,179
Write something that sounds

32
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:55,979
right and maybe it gets marked

33
00:00:55,980 --> 00:00:56,360
correct.

34
00:00:57,420 --> 00:00:58,460
So three questions.

35
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:00,779
Why does AI make things up at

36
00:01:00,780 --> 00:01:01,020
all?

37
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:02,840
Why does it sound so sure?

38
00:01:03,620 --> 00:01:04,860
And how do you protect yourself?

39
00:01:05,667 --> 00:01:07,966
Question one, why make things up

40
00:01:07,967 --> 00:01:08,407
at all?

41
00:01:09,407 --> 00:01:10,747
Because of how it gets marked,

42
00:01:11,407 --> 00:01:13,866
in 2025, researchers at

43
00:01:13,867 --> 00:01:16,286
OpenAI, the company behind

44
00:01:16,287 --> 00:01:18,626
ChatGPT, published a paper

45
00:01:18,627 --> 00:01:20,366
called Why Language Models

46
00:01:20,367 --> 00:01:20,987
Hallucinate.

47
00:01:21,627 --> 00:01:23,027
Their answer is that exam.

48
00:01:24,007 --> 00:01:25,827
When AIs are trained and tested,

49
00:01:26,327 --> 00:01:27,666
most of the scoring works the

50
00:01:27,667 --> 00:01:28,207
same way.

51
00:01:28,647 --> 00:01:30,986
A right answer gets a point, a

52
00:01:30,987 --> 00:01:33,186
wrong answer gets zero, and I

53
00:01:33,187 --> 00:01:34,687
don't know, also gets zero.

54
00:01:35,227 --> 00:01:36,807
So bluffing always wins.

55
00:01:37,887 --> 00:01:39,006
And some facts it has barely

56
00:01:39,007 --> 00:01:39,467
seen.

57
00:01:40,047 --> 00:01:42,306
The researchers asked one AI for

58
00:01:42,307 --> 00:01:44,006
one of their birthdays and told

59
00:01:44,007 --> 00:01:45,647
it to answer only if it knew.

60
00:01:46,207 --> 00:01:48,266
Three tries, three different

61
00:01:48,267 --> 00:01:49,987
dates, all wrong.

62
00:01:54,067 --> 00:01:56,206
Question two, why does it sound

63
00:01:56,207 --> 00:01:56,747
so sure?

64
00:01:57,847 --> 00:01:59,346
Remember, it's always guessing

65
00:01:59,347 --> 00:02:00,327
what sounds right.

66
00:02:00,767 --> 00:02:02,506
A real fact and a made up one

67
00:02:02,507 --> 00:02:04,366
come out of the same machine, in

68
00:02:04,367 --> 00:02:06,387
the same smooth, friendly voice.

69
00:02:06,947 --> 00:02:08,226
There's no wobble when it's

70
00:02:08,227 --> 00:02:08,527
wrong.

71
00:02:09,407 --> 00:02:10,966
And its training to be a helpful

72
00:02:10,967 --> 00:02:12,466
assistant was marked the same

73
00:02:12,467 --> 00:02:12,807
way.

74
00:02:13,247 --> 00:02:15,066
A sure answer beats, I'm not

75
00:02:15,067 --> 00:02:15,387
sure.

76
00:02:16,327 --> 00:02:18,086
That's how an airline's chatbot

77
00:02:18,087 --> 00:02:20,126
in Canada told a man whose

78
00:02:20,127 --> 00:02:22,166
grandmother had just died that

79
00:02:22,167 --> 00:02:23,786
he could buy a full price ticket

80
00:02:23,787 --> 00:02:25,706
and claim a family funeral

81
00:02:25,707 --> 00:02:26,627
discount later.

82
00:02:27,247 --> 00:02:28,786
The airline's own website said

83
00:02:28,787 --> 00:02:29,267
the opposite.

84
00:02:30,387 --> 00:02:32,826
In 2024, a tribunal made the

85
00:02:32,827 --> 00:02:33,587
airline pay.

86
00:02:34,127 --> 00:02:35,447
Its chatbot, its mistake.

87
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:38,239
Question three, how do you

88
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:39,040
protect yourself?

89
00:02:39,680 --> 00:02:40,320
Three habits.

90
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:43,499
One, ask for sources and then

91
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:44,580
actually open them.

92
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:45,900
Remember the lawyer?

93
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:48,239
He asked for cases and got

94
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:48,700
cases.

95
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:50,500
They just weren't real.

96
00:02:51,140 --> 00:02:52,159
A link you haven't clicked

97
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:52,960
proves nothing.

98
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:55,760
Two, make it an open book exam.

99
00:02:56,740 --> 00:02:58,099
Paste in the real thing, the

100
00:02:58,100 --> 00:02:59,860
letter, the webpage, the rules,

101
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:01,859
and ask it to answer only from

102
00:03:01,860 --> 00:03:02,140
that.

103
00:03:02,620 --> 00:03:04,319
It guesses far less when the

104
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:05,819
answer is right in front of it.

105
00:03:05,820 --> 00:03:08,280
Far less, not never.

106
00:03:09,460 --> 00:03:11,099
Three, double check the details

107
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:12,360
that are easiest to bluff.

108
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:14,579
Names, numbers, dates, and

109
00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:14,800
quotes.

110
00:03:15,533 --> 00:03:17,353
So why does AI hallucinate?

111
00:03:18,273 --> 00:03:19,892
It's a guesser, marked like a

112
00:03:19,893 --> 00:03:21,612
student in an exam where a blank

113
00:03:21,613 --> 00:03:22,413
scores zero.

114
00:03:22,993 --> 00:03:23,653
So it bluffs.

115
00:03:24,333 --> 00:03:26,252
And right answers and wrong ones

116
00:03:26,253 --> 00:03:27,772
come out in the same confident

117
00:03:27,773 --> 00:03:28,253
voice.

118
00:03:28,753 --> 00:03:29,732
So you can't hear the

119
00:03:29,733 --> 00:03:30,133
difference.

120
00:03:31,233 --> 00:03:32,892
Sounding sure is not the same as

121
00:03:32,893 --> 00:03:33,413
being right.

122
00:03:34,353 --> 00:03:35,932
So treat it like a very fast

123
00:03:35,933 --> 00:03:37,493
student, not an expert.

124
00:03:38,093 --> 00:03:40,112
Give it the book and check its

125
00:03:40,113 --> 00:03:40,393
work.

126
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:43,080
But so far, AI only talks.

127
00:03:43,720 --> 00:03:45,339
What happens when it can act and

128
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:47,020
book the flight, send the email,

129
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:47,860
spend the money?

130
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:50,239
That's called an AI agent, and

131
00:03:50,240 --> 00:03:50,720
it's next.

132
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:53,320
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
